    description: "Concept DOI, resolves to the latest version"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Nhat-Nguyet/nhatnguyet-lunar-ziwei-bazi-numerology"
url: "https://nhatnguyet.org/en/ma-nguon-mo"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  TypeScript and Python libraries, a command line tool, an MCP server and 5
  web components for East Asian lunisolar calendars and the traditions
  computed on them: lunar dates for China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Macau,
  Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia, the Philippines, Korea, Japan and Vietnam,
  each on its own time zone, the 24 solar terms, auspicious hours, Zi Wei Dou
  Shu, BaZi, Pythagorean numerology, the I Ching (hexagrams, Six Lines najia
  charts, the Gua Qi daily hexagram), the feng shui rules of traditional
  schools (Eight Mansions, Xuan Kong flying stars, Luban rulers) and a dataset
  of 206 Vietnamese festivals, with an English API, results in English,
  Chinese and Vietnamese, and named school options where the books disagree.
  Every result reproduces the engine behind nhatnguyet.org (engine 2.3.0) and
  is checked by 17,291 core test vectors (28,586 including school options,
  auspicious hours, I Ching and feng shui). The calendar is checked against
  NASA new moons and IMCCE solar terms from 1900 to 2100. With the same rules,
  Hanoi and Beijing time give a different lunar date on 2,814 of those 73,414
  days, Seoul and Beijing time on 2,818; a library that computes only the
  Chinese calendar gives the wrong Vietnamese date on 2,724. Code is MIT;
  calendar data and test vectors are CC BY 4.0; the reading and knowledge
  datasets are CC BY-NC 4.0 from 20 November 2026.
